{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1237380-what-man-was-not-meant-to-know","id":1237380,"title":"What Man Was Not Meant To Know","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-07-20T19:57:57-07:00","id":1237380,"title":"What Man Was Not Meant To Know","content":"So, one of the classic complaints about 2e is that it overexplained things, making canon what was meant to be ambiguous. <br /> <br /> Well, nothing we forumites say is canon, so I'd like to take a crack at some weird and esoteric setting details and see what we come up with. Questions like: <br /> <br /> * What was/is Zen-Mu like?<br /> * What (besides the Contagion) has the Dowager pulled out of the Well of Udr? Who built that thing, anyway?<br /> * What is the Shining Answer? Has Gaia found it yet?<br /> * What were some of the other Primordial races from the Age of Glories? Do they still exist in the Underworld like the Alaun?<br /> * Does Cemunian's world still exist somewhere out in the Wyld? Are there other renegade Primordials that didn't want to play ball with Theion?<br /> * If you dig deep enough into Creation's soil, what do you find? Beneath the Darkbrood lairs, obviously.<br /> <br /> I know this is zooming out to a celestial scale a bit more than we're supposed to as of 3e, but I feel like ditching all the weird, cosmic esoterica would be doing a grave disservice to the game and removing some of what makes Creation so imaginative and engaging.<br /> <br /> Besides, any Twilight worth her salt has got to ask some of these questions eventually, right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T20:36:21-07:00","id":1237388,"title":"","content":"One of the problems with Zen-Mu is that it predates strict causality. Like, there is a point in time, in the history of Creation, beyond which time exists as we understand it - <i>cause</i> precedes <i>effect</i>. Things occur in the shadow of what came before.<br /> <br /> Zen-Mu did not work like this. In Zen-Mu, causality could run in loops. One thing could cause a second thing which caused a third thing which caused the first thing. It was 'mythic' time, preceding reality as we understand it.<br /> <br /> Zen-Mu is fundamentally just not going to make any sense to us. In the same regard that all stories with time travel in them inevitably become stories ABOUT time travel, any attempt to plumb the history of Zen-Mu - the nothing-nothing - is going to run in to this problem and come to a hard stop.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T20:59:13-07:00","id":1237393,"title":"","content":"I vehemently disagree with the EX3 thesis that explanation of the First Age by definition diminishes the First Age. Detailing it has been called &quot;Creating an unavoidable blueprint for Ideal Creation&quot; and I couldn't possibly disagree more. To me, there is a functional difference between saying &quot;The Emissary is either A/B/C/D or E&quot; and &quot;The Order-Conferring Trade Pattern used the movement of jade through the hands of mortals, by means of commercial interest, as one of the main buffers against the Wyld.<br /> <br /> The first is simply reductive. It takes away options as to what the Emissary might be, while really giving us nothing positive in exchange. The second, OTOH, gives us an EPIC example of how the then-Lords of Creation were so puissant, they could make something as mundane as commerce into a mighty bulwark against the howling madness of the Primal Chaos. It doesn't mandate that a Second Age Eclipse devoted to strengthening Creation at its edges use &quot;Super Banking Prana&quot; to accomplish his/her goals. It provides lore that, assuming hypothetical Eclipse wishes to seek answers to the present challenge in the past successes of others so focused, can search for, learn to refine, or even repurpose for his own ends. It's additive. This whole &quot;If we write it, we're insulting or diminishing the creative opportunities available to Storytellers and Players&quot; is, to me, a high-sounding rationale for a style change they wish to make, but feel the need to justify.<br /> <br /> <b>More on-topic:</b> I would've LOVED more info on the Island Kingdom of Bioengineers that the Solar Deliberative destroyed, which was mentioned in MoEP: Infernals. Would've loved accounts of the Time of Cascading Years experiences of those Elder Exalts who survive into the Second Age. Desperately wanted more detail on the Solar (and Lunar) reaction to Facet Raven's death of old age, and the resultant social impacts.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, I felt like the fate of the People of the Forest never got nearly the attention other Created Races did. The Pelagials got this huge section, but the Forest People get some mention of their activities while still slaves of the Deliberative, some mention why they weren't kitted out with the longevity the Peoples of the Air, Sea and Earth were all given, and...nada. The Dune People were much less interesting to me, but they got quite a lot of coverage in comparison.<br /> <br /> Hmm, Shogunate infrastructure would've been wonderful. We get a basic thesis that the Shogunate was basically living off the inherited Celestial Exalt-created infrastructure, but surely they had SOME independent achievements that were Wow-Worthy.<br /> <br /> Some hint why Mercury pushed Gen Iza to take off into the Wyld. It didn't need to be spelled out, but IF there had been a linkage with the Usurpation to come, and a role for a returning Gen Iza in the Second Age to play, I would've eaten that up! (That thesis is currently a major element in a story I'm writing over at The Freedom Stone, which still exists, if anyone is interested. Great stories there.)<br /> <br /> Given the literally potentially Creation-ending existential threats many of the older First Age Exalts had fought personally, I would've lived to see some kind of &quot;Doomsday Prepping&quot; on an individual level. Not just some &quot;loot cache to be looted&quot;...but something in some remote place, with a sort of time-capsule feel to it. Like Opal, if it'd been deliberate rather than the Slayer of Armies turning it into &quot;Evacuate and stay gone, or be annihilated&quot; (Going to Opal with 5-7 other Celestial Exalts, also part of my current story.)<br /> <br /> More Creation-born lost Wyld Outposts. A write-up for Xarlath (where the giant sentient bees, who only come to Creation from the Wyld for nectar every so often to make their super-honey, come from).<br /> <br /> More interesting locales in the Underworld. We got some good ones, but more is better.<br /> <br /> How's that for a start?<br /> <br /> Edit: Info, such as time-lines, write-ups of some major events during, and write-ups for cultures which emerged post-Usurpation, but didn't survive the Contagion/Crusade would've been wonderful. For the most part, Creation's archeology Re: Ruins has been &quot;First Age Badass Stuff, Hand-Me-Down First Age Shogunate Stuff, Second Age.&quot; The Shogunate persisted for centuries. It was the Bronze Faction's supposed wet dream, before the inevitable and utterly foreseeable consequence of pink-slipping 6/7ths of the Celestial Exalts hit home. Supposedly, lots happened during the Shogunate. I'd just like to know more of it. (Similarly, given we know there were violent, military-based successions in Shoguns, an answer for how none of those civil wars ever provided opportunity for either the few free Solar Exaltations, or any Lunars to get Threshold-entrenched would've been good. I know the answer now is that's exactly where some of the Lunar Dominions come from, and it's one of the few parts of EX3 I really love, but still)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-21-2018, 12:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T10:32:58-07:00","id":1239728,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve always been intrigued by Zen-Mu, too, especially the line about the Primordials \u201cfleeing it in despair.\u201d No mention was made of any sort of super-Primordial that drove them out, or anything, nor is there mention of any sort of tragic loss or injury suffered there. It just seems that the reality built by Primordials, for Primordials, and inhabited only by Primordials was enough to make them absolutely miserable.<br /> <br /> In pondering why that might be, it occurred to me that two dozen-plus super-entities, each of whom was infinitely powerful within its own defining concept, but also infinitely self-absorbed, and utterly incapable of working together, would definitely cock up their first attempt at universe-building. Imagine the Primordials, circled around a cauldron, each one contributing one absolute metaphysical law as an ingredient to a bubbling reality stew, without even the slightest notion of coordination, teamwork, harmony or compromise. Then imagine them having to live in that mess, realizing that existence itself, as crafted by their own hands, was an awful, kludgy, error-ridden ruin that seemed determined to grind you down under the weight of its catastrophically poor design. That might be enough to drive even the Titans away in despair, no?<br /> <br /> In the last Exalted game I played in (\u201cMaybe Gem Won\u2019t Explode\u201d), I built a custom 2.5 Sidereal Charm around the idea of Zen-Mu being inherently oppressive towards the concepts of action, agency and accomplishment. The fluff involved overlaying some of Zen-Mu\u2019s physical/metaphysical laws and properties on top of Creation. The crunch involved taking all of the least player-friendly rules and game mechanics from as many White-Wolf RPG editions as I could find, and stacking them on top of each other as cruelly as I could. Here\u2019s the result, cut \u2018n pasted from my original Forum post, back in January.<br /> <br /> <b>ON ZEN-MU, SIDEREALS AND THE SPELL THAT WAS TRADED FOR A CHARM</b><br /> <br /> Before the Primordials ever wrenched form out of chaos, before the inspiration of Yu Shan and the engineering of Creation, the Ancient Ones dwelt for a time in the realm of Zen-Mu, built as a bulwark against the annoyances of the Wyld\u2019s endless hunger. They eventually grew weary of Zen-Mu, and despair led them to abandon the realm they had built, to seek the Shining Answer elsewhere. Abandoned Zen-Mu became Forsaken Zen-Mu, and then Forgotten Zen-Mu.<br /> <br /> But Zen-Mu yet exists. Ever since the wake of the Time of Cascading Years, the Sidereal Exalted have suspected that the engineering of Creation was informed by lessons learned from some sort of earlier prototype of reality, and their Theanoan statistical modeling supported such theory. Intrigued, they pored over every account of conversation with Autochthon, Gaia, and the lesser souls of the Yozis. Gradually, the Fivescore Fellowship pieced together an understanding of Zen-Mu, its antagonistic metaphysical principles, and how residing there drove the Primordials to despair.<br /> <br /> By the time the Sidereals began to contemplate the Usurpation, the mathemagicians of the Forbidding Manse of Ivy\u2019s school of Sorcery, Dept. 137, were nearing completion of a Sapphire Circle spell that would render a fragment of Creation temporarily coterminous with the laws of Zen-Mu, tainting chance and probability for whatever poor wretch that suffered its effects. <br /> <br /> When the Maiden of Secrets herself manifested before the Sorcerers, demanding that they cease all such research and surrender the unfinished spell to her, one among the Viziers was ready. Prydonian Scholar, widely regarded as the craftiest of Jupiter\u2019s own Chosen, had anticipated her objection to crafting such a spell, and was prepared with a counter-offer: Allow her Chosen to manifest the effect as a Sidereal Occult Charm, and they would surrender the spell, along with all memory of how they developed it. Thus, the ability to summon Zen-Mu into Creation would remain only with the Sidereals.<br /> <br /> For her own reasons, the Maiden of Secrets agreed Thus was born the Charm: <b>Invocation of Zen-Mu</b>.<br /> <br /> Only a few Sidereals were taught the Charm prior to the Usurpation, and in the chaos that followed the Breaking of the Mask, Jupiter quietly sealed the Invocation of Zen-Mu away from the Sidereal Charm set. All those who had been taught the Charm had perished in the Usurpation, and all knowledge of the Invocation\u2019s existence faded from memory as completely as the spell-turned-Charm fell from use. <br /> <br /> But the Time of Tumult has engulfed Creation. Factions and fellows who have long stood in opposition have begun to relinquish old enmities in the face of threats, both new and resurgent. Mighty beings, long accustomed to unilateral action, have been compelled to seek counsel and share troubling observations with others of like stature. And ancient prohibitions, once held to be absolute, are beginning to be lifted. In particular, revelations regarding the existence and nature of the Infernal Exalted are the things most likely to spur Jupiter to restore to the Sidereals the Charm known as Invocation of Zen-Mu.<br /> <br /> <b>\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014</b><br /> <br /> Zen-Mu is not in any way sentient, but it has strong antipathies nonetheless. Just as Creation itself abhors any vacuum, literal, symbolic, or metaphorical, Zen-Mu despises accomplishment. Where most actions in Creation spawn equal, opposite reactions, achievements in Zen-Mu spawn repercussions. Its elements of randomness lead to stagnating sameness, and yet its unyielding laws infect every principle with uncertainty.<br /> <br /> All of this is both by design, and entirely unintended. The Primordials had never before considered working together in any sort of productive harmony, for the very concept was antithetical to their nature. And so the crafting of Zen-Mu, the first joint effort of the mighty Primordials, largely consisted of each of them adding one unyielding principle to its design, without any thought of how their own absolutist metaphysics might interact with the those of their siblings.<br /> <br /> The result was...not what it might have otherwise been.<br /> <br /> The Primordials were beings of the purest power, and yet existence within Zen-Mu drove them to despair, eventually sending them fleeing into the Wyld. And if the Primordials and their most treasured souls could find only misery in Zen-Mu, then the suffering of any lesser being that might labor under that realm\u2019s restrictions would be legendary...even if they were Exalted.<br /> <br /> <b>Invocation of Zen-Mu</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b> 15m, 1wp<br /> <b>Minimums:</b> Essence 5, Occult 5<br /> <b>Type:</b>Simple<br /> <br /> <b>Keywords:</b> Combo-OK, Obvious, Theanoan<br /> <b>Duration:</b> One scene<br /> <b>Prerequisite Charms:</b> Sapphire Circle Sorcery<br /> <br /> The Sidereal brings to mind the metaphysical principles that defined Zen-Mu, visualizing the geometric patterns formed by the interconnections between them. She then rotates this pattern, attempting to find an angle that allows it to fit over the pattern of Creation\u2019s principles. <br /> <br /> The Sidereal rolls (Intelligence + Occult) against a difficulty of (target\u2019s Essence), adding her own Essence in automatic successes. If successful, the user clicks the theoretical model of Zen-Mu\u2019s principles over Creation\u2019s laws, imposing the harsh, unforgiving strictures and demands of that forsaken realm upon her for until her DV has refreshed a number of times equal to the successes rolled. This is not a Shaping effect, and cannot be resisted with Shaping defenses.<br /> <br /> Players whose characters who suffer this Charm\u2019s effect apply a number of changes to how they roll dice, count successes, and interpret results.<br /> <br /> <i>Apply the following changes to every die roll made by a tainted character, including damage rolls:</i><br /> <br /> <b>* Raise the standard target number of all rolls to [8].</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> In walling themselves off from the Wyld, the Holy Tyrant knew that his brothers and sisters would have only each other to struggle against. Tyrant though he was, he was also their King, and and he alone bore responsibility for the well-being of his subjects. Therefore, he ensured that his siblings\u2019 Primordial might could not be turned against their fellows without focus, effort, and intent.<br /> <br /> <b>* Count any die that rolls a [10] as only one success, not two.</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> The Principle of Hierarchy could not countenance the thought of those with less raw ability and inferior skills matching or outperforming their betters due to luck, or mere affinity. Zen-Mu was designed to always give those of greater power the greater chance of success.<br /> <br /> <b>* Every die that rolls [1] subtracts one success from that roll\u2019s total successes.</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> Cecyline knew that she and her siblings were perfect, and therefore the refuge they built would be perfect. As such, she contributed the decree that all attempts to enact change upon such perfection, or even do anything but exist within Zen-Mu\u2019s blessed borders, should be opposed by Zen-Mu itself. <br /> <br /> <b>* If more dice show [1] than [8+] on any roll, that action botches.</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> The Dragon\u2019s Shadow knew he could not attain the transcendent state of antithesis he sought while limited to the stifling confines of Zen-Mu. For his contribution, he poured into Zen-Mu\u2019s design his own hatred of the accomplishments of others, in the hope that the resultant frustrations would eventually drive the Primordial host to abandon it for a more desirable home.<br /> <br /> <b>* Effects that normally grant automatic successes instead add two extra dice per automatic success.</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> Isidoros, who despises the thought of relying upon anything other than his own might, rammed and trampled the conceptual pillars of Zen-Mu until he had beaten all traces of constancy, reliability, and other such impurities out of its very design. Such absolute uncertainty forced its inhabitants to actively pit their might against every challenge that arose, making existence in Zen-Mu a constant series of tests.<br /> <br /> <b>* If an effect allows a re-roll, any dice that showed [1] on the original roll carry over to the re-roll, and are applied to the new roll.</b><br /> <b>Reason:</b> To Adri\u00e1n, the River of All Torments, the thought of abandoning existence on the razor\u2019s edge of chance, action and consequence, in favor of cleaving unto the stale predetermined outcomes of Essence Resurgent was horrifying. Determined to spare her siblings the deprivation of existence without peril, she scoured all elasticity from Zen-Mu\u2019s meta-structure, eliminating any possibility of second chances.<br /> <br /> Note that the universal principles summoned via the Invocation of Zen-Mu override the Sidereal Astrology of Creation. A victim tainted by the Invocation cannot be the target of any Ascending or Descending Destinies, and any pre-existing Destinies are suppressed for the duration of the Charm.<br /> <br /> The Theanoan keyword indicates that only Sidereals who have studied Sorcery under the paradigm of Theano may learn this Charm. Currently, the only place Theanoan Sorcery is taught is Dept. 137 of the Forbidding Manse of Ivy, in Yu Shan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1536-39steps","name":"39Steps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1536_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"255"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T10:41:29-07:00","id":1239729,"title":"","content":"That is absolutely not a sidereal charm. It is something that is wholly outside the loom. It would make much more sense for a general Infernal charm or sorcery but it's not something that could be woven into the loom. Due to how antithical it is to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T11:22:11-07:00","id":1239740,"title":"","content":"Zen Mu I had inhabited by strange creatures(Proto-Elemental Dragons that more resemble Flowey than anything else, a giant of red coral that grows when in contact with water until he shatters into smaller versions and sees people as their creations, and so forth). I'm looking forward to learning more perhaps with that gate in the Dream Sea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T11:42:54-07:00","id":1239744,"title":"","content":"Now that the Getimians exist, that sounds like something more suited to them -- not expressing the subtleties of the world and Heaven's design, but rather breaking or subverting them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>But sexually.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1797-ellis","name":"Ellis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1797_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"535"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T13:08:27-07:00","id":1239774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ellis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1237380-what-man-was-not-meant-to-know?p=1239744#post1239744\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now that the Getimians exist, that sounds like something more suited to them -- not expressing the subtleties of the world and Heaven's design, but rather breaking or subverting them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. This seems like exactly the kind of knowledge Rakan Thulio would have absconded with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T13:34:12-07:00","id":1239785,"title":"","content":"Some stuff 3rd edition has already addressed.<br /> -Zen-Mu has been referenced twice so far (the portal in Palanquin and Karvara)<br /> -The core introduced two new races in the form of the Backbenders and the gigantes of Dis, described as pre-human and primordial respectively.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T13:52:31-07:00","id":1239792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1237380-what-man-was-not-meant-to-know?p=1239785#post1239785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some stuff 3rd edition has already addressed.<br /> -Zen-Mu has been referenced twice so far (the portal in Palanquin and Karvara)<br /> -The core introduced two new races in the form of the Backbenders and the gigantes of Dis, described as pre-human and primordial respectively.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> To expand, the Backbenders we know are surgeons and the gigantes are slavers who IIRC employ soul stones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/>